{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"Given the conditions:\n\\[ x^2 + y^2 = 7 \\]\n\\[ x^3 + y^3 = 10 \\]\n\nWe need to find all possible values for \\(x + y\\).\n\nFirst, recall the identity for the sum of cubes:\n\\[ x^3 + y^3 = (x+y)(x^2 - xy + y^2) \\]\n\nSubstitute \\(x^2 + y^2 = 7\\) into the identity:\n\\[ x^3 + y^3 = (x+y)(7 - xy) \\]\n\nWe know \\(x^3 + y^3 = 10\\), so:\n\\[ 10 = (x+y)(7 - xy) \\]\n\nLet \\(s = x + y\\) and \\(p = xy\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[ 10 = s(7 - p) \\]\n\\[ p = 7 - \\frac{10}{s} \\]\n\nNext, we use the identity for the sum of squares:\n\\[ x^2 + y^2 = (x+y)^2 - 2xy \\]\n\\[ 7 = s^2 - 2p \\]\n\nSubstitute \\(p = 7 - \\frac{10}{s}\\) into the equation:\n\\[ 7 = s^2 - 2\\left(7 - \\frac{10}{s}\\right) \\]\n\\[ 7 = s^2 - 14 + \\frac{20}{s} \\]\n\\[ s^2 + \\frac{20}{s} - 14 - 7 = 0 \\]\n\\[ s^2 + \\frac{20}{s} - 21 = 0 \\]\n\nMultiply through by \\(s\\) to clear the fraction:\n\\[ s^3 + 20 - 21s = 0 \\]\n\\[ s^3 - 21s + 20 = 0 \\]\n\nWe solve this cubic equation for \\(s\\). We can use the Rational Root Theorem to test possible rational roots. The possible rational roots are the factors of the constant term (20) divided by the factors of the leading coefficient (1), which are:\n\\[ \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4, \\pm 5, \\pm 10, \\pm 20 \\]\n\nTesting these values:\nFor \\(s = 1\\):\n\\[ 1^3 - 21(1) + 20 = 1 - 21 + 20 = 0 \\]\nSo, \\(s = 1\\) is a root.\n\n","prefix_end":1265,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[22043,279,4682,510,78045,856,61,17,488,379,61,17,284,220,22,1124,921,78045,856,61,18,488,379,61,18,284,220,16,15,1124,2533,1654,1184,311,1477,678,3204,2750,369,17767,87,488,379,59,3593,5338,11,19091,279,9569,369,279,2629,315,54104,510,78045,856,61,18,488,379,61,18,284,320,87,43010,2376,87,61,17,481,30784,488,379,61,17,8,1124,2533,3136,7660,17767,87,61,17,488,379,61,17,284,220,22,57758,1119,279,9569,510,78045,85